WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:07.670
Marvel Comics hasn't always been as big as
it is today, and not all of the company's

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:11.700
history is as heroic as the adventures of
Spider-Man or Captain America.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.900
Since starting out as Timely Comics in 1939,
they've created countless classics, but they've

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.000
also done a few things they probably wish
we'd forget.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.930
Here are a few of Marvel's biggest missteps.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:24.940
Atlas distribution disaster

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:29.640
Back in the 1950s when Marvel was still called
Atlas Comics, publisher Martin Goodman decided

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.450
to outsource distribution.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:35.570
One problem: the distribution company almost
immediately went bankrupt, leaving Goodman

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:39.450
no option but to ask arch-rival DC to distribute
his books.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.940
DC put the screws to Atlas, limiting them
to only eight comics a month—a huge drop

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:47.410
from the 60 or so titles Atlas had previously
published.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:51.790
Not only did profits plunge, but Atlas had
to fire almost all of their artists and writers.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:54.330
There was simply nothing for them to do.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:58.250
Ironically, the company survived because of
an editorial oversight by Stan Lee.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:02.079
For years, he had been buying too many stories
from his creators, ending up with a closet

00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:03.860
full of unpublished comics.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:08.320
When Atlas imploded, they were able to stay
afloat by finally publishing the issues Stan

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.220
had accidentally stockpiled.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:11.620
Tossing pages

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:15.930
Before comics were seen as collectibles, original
comic art was a means to an end.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:20.030
It was photographed, colored, printed…and
then the originals were discarded.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:24.100
While some original pages have been preserved
by collectors, Marvel spent decades simply

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:28.520
chucking countless others, not unlike the
way the BBC tossed dozens of early Doctor

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:29.630
Who episodes.

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:35.340
Today, finding a missing comic page is a victory
for both collectors and comics historians.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:36.340
Creator mistreatment

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:40.760
It’s a sad fact that while Marvel rakes
in billions of dollars worldwide with their

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.850
films, the creators often see little or no
money from it thanks to "work for hire" contracts

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.250
that sign all the rights away.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:51.159
One of the worst examples of Marvel's questionable
treatment of their creators is the case of

00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:54.240
Gary Friedrich, the co-creator of Ghost Rider.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.009
After the Ghost Rider movies came out, Friedrich
took Marvel to court for compensation related

00:01:58.009 --> 00:01:59.400
to merchandising.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.810
Not only did he lose the lawsuit, Marvel countersued
for $17,000 and forced him to stop selling

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:06.860
anything Ghost Rider-related at conventions.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:11.129
A judge later overturned the decision and
both parties settled out of court.

00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:12.129
Royal Roy

00:02:12.129 --> 00:02:16.100
For a period, Marvel Comics also published
Star Comics, a label dedicated to animated

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:19.890
tie-ins like ThunderCats and Fraggle Rock
and other kid-friendly fare.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:23.040
Part of Star's approach was to copy the success
of Harvey Comics.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.640
They did it a little too well, though.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.260
Royal Roy, a young millionaire, was basically
an exact copy of Harvey's Richie Rich, and

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.020
was actually created by an ex-Harvey employee.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:35.890
Harvey sued Marvel for infringing on their
property, and Royal Roy was canceled after

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:37.379
a handful of issues.

00:02:37.379 --> 00:02:38.590
Ron Perelman

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:43.770
In January of 1989, Ron Perelman bought Marvel
for just over $82 million.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:47.360
He then went full on Wolf of Wall Street,
using Marvel as collateral to leverage the

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:52.790
purchase of other trading card, comic book,
and toy companies...sending the company $700

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:54.349
million into debt.

00:02:54.349 --> 00:02:59.490
"Hi yi, hmmmm, hi yi, hmmmm, hmmmm"

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:03.530
By the time Perelman's reign ended, the company
had declared bankruptcy, over a third of its

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:07.780
employees had been laid off, and the entire
comic book industry had collapsed.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:08.780
And Perelman?

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.840
Well, he got rich off of it all, with an estimated
net worth of $14 billion.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.570
Sometimes the supervillains win.

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:16.680
Film Rights

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:20.599
In the early '90s, cash-strapped Marvel raised
capital by signing away the film rights to

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:23.180
pretty much all their characters for pennies
on the dollar.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:27.350
It seems short-sighted, but it almost makes
sense given that every Marvel film prior to

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:29.379
1998's Blade was a failure.

00:03:29.379 --> 00:03:30.790
Don't remember those films?

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:32.400
There's a good reason for that.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.440
First there was the 1990 direct-to-video version
of Captain America, which reported profits

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:40.270
of just over $10,000 on a $10 million budget.

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:42.450
"Holy mackerel"

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:46.830
Then, four years later, the first Fantastic
Four was produced on a shoestring just so

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:50.370
a small movie studio could legally retain
the rights to the characters.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:55.120
It was so bad, Marvel ended up spending millions
of dollars to keep the film from being distributed

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:59.330
in order to save the brand from being cheapened
by a low-budget flop.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:03.099
Marvel learned their lesson, though, and in
2004 they formed their own studio and began

00:04:03.099 --> 00:04:07.260
buying back the rights to their characters
so they could make the movies themselves.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:10.099
Seems like that move worked out pretty well.

00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:11.099
Marvel's Image

00:04:11.099 --> 00:04:15.129
In 1992, several of Marvel's top creators
went to their bosses and asked for a better

00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:19.509
deal: more money per page, more royalties
for characters they created, and a more fair

00:04:19.509 --> 00:04:22.449
portion of what Marvel was earning off of
their work.

00:04:22.449 --> 00:04:24.800
Marvel told them to pound sand.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.729
In response, the artists quit Marvel and formed
their own company: Image Comics.

00:04:28.729 --> 00:04:32.539
They became superstars, and Image went on
to revolutionize the industry, while Marvel's

00:04:32.539 --> 00:04:36.389
stock dropped so sharply it was featured on
an episode of Moneyline.

00:04:36.389 --> 00:04:39.349
Marvel survived, but they could have stayed
ahead of the game—and saved themselves a

00:04:39.349 --> 00:04:41.909
lot of trouble—just by playing fair.

00:04:41.909 --> 00:04:42.909
Guiding Light

00:04:42.909 --> 00:04:47.539
In 2006, Marvel's Editor-in-Chief Joe Quesada
thought it would be a great idea to partner

00:04:47.539 --> 00:04:51.719
with, of all companies, Procter &amp; Gamble,
mostly known for selling Pringles, creating

00:04:51.719 --> 00:04:56.009
the bowel-loosening food additive Olestra—and
producing the soap opera Guiding Light.

00:04:56.009 --> 00:05:00.020
To that end, Guiding Light aired an episode
in which a long-time character was shocked

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:03.719
by some Halloween decorations and briefly
gained superpowers.

00:05:03.719 --> 00:05:07.099
Marvel wrote their own version of the story
as a six-page mini-crossover.

00:05:07.099 --> 00:05:10.529
The hope was that the cross-promotion would
encourage soap viewers and comics fans to

00:05:10.529 --> 00:05:11.999
walk on the other side.

00:05:11.999 --> 00:05:13.130
But not many did.

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:16.060
And Guiding Light only lasted a few more years
before it was canceled.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:17.060
Dang.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:18.110
Thanks for watching!

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:20.930
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:24.709
And leave us a comment to let us know which
Marvel mistake you think was the worst...

